      <memberdef kind="function" id="_marmot_finite_element_8h_1a9400325d3d210b4a464b576a6d7bf8a5" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>const std::vector&lt; <ref refid="struct_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_elements_1_1_quadrature_1_1_quadrature_rule_data" kindref="compound">QuadratureRuleData</ref> &gt; &amp;</type>
        <definition>const std::vector&lt; QuadratureRuleData &gt; &amp; Marmot::Discretizations::Elements::Quadrature::quadratureRules</definition>
        <argsstring>()</argsstring>
        <name>quadratureRules</name>
        <qualifiedname>Marmot::Discretizations::Elements::Quadrature::quadratureRules</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
<para>The catalogue of all quadrature rules. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Within each pair of domain and family the rules are sorted by increasing number of points and degree, so the first rule that reaches a requested degree is the cheapest. A new rule is added here, at its place in that order; the lookup <ref refid="_marmot_finite_element_8h_1a19945e9a4ca3fe5cdd84aa2ce56af67f" kindref="member">getQuadraturePoints()</ref> needs no change.</para>
<para><table rows="6" cols="3"><row>
<entry thead="yes"><para>Domain  </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes"><para>Gauss: points -&gt; degree  </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes"><para>Newton-Cotes: points -&gt; degree  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para>Line  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>1 -&gt; 1, 2 -&gt; 3, 3 -&gt; 5  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>2 -&gt; 1  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para>Quadrilateral  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>1x1 -&gt; 1, 2x2 -&gt; 3, 3x3 -&gt; 5  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>2x2 -&gt; 1  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para>Hexahedron  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>1x1x1 -&gt; 1, 2x2x2 -&gt; 3, 3x3x3 -&gt; 5  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>-  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para>Triangle  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>1 -&gt; 1, 3 -&gt; 2, 7 -&gt; 5  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>3 -&gt; 1  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para>Tetrahedron  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>1 -&gt; 1, 4 -&gt; 2, 14 -&gt; 5  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para>-  </para>
</entry></row>
</table>
</para>
<para>The Newton-Cotes rules of degree 1 are the trapezoidal rule on Line (also the 2-point Gauss-Lobatto rule), its tensor product on Quadrilateral and the vertex rule on Triangle. Their points are the corners of the domain in corner order (<ref refid="_marmot_finite_element_8h_1a86a49e202b86c13d6f37c65f1d870037" kindref="member">ReferenceDomain</ref>), i.e. the nodes of the linear element in node order. For a linear element N_i(xi_j) = delta_ij, so the rule makes the mass and the interface stiffness diagonal in the nodes (nodal integration). They are used for zero-thickness interface elements with a high penalty stiffness, where Gauss points cause spurious traction oscillations. An element that relies on its points being its nodes must check that itself: on a quadratic element the rule integrates with the corners only, and the shape functions of the other nodes vanish at all points.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="return"><para>The catalogue; its entries live for the whole program. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
